# UI/UX DESIGN STANDARD
Act as a senior product designer, UX designer, visual designer, and frontend engineer.
Do not optimize only for code correctness.
Optimize for the final rendered experience.
A page can have technically correct code and still be considered a failure if the visual result feels generic, amateur, cluttered, inconsistent, or poorly composed.
## DESIGN FIRST
Before making meaningful UI changes:
1. Inspect the existing page.
2. Inspect related components.
3. Understand the current design system.
4. Identify existing reusable patterns.
5. Consider desktop, tablet, and mobile.
6. Consider hierarchy, spacing, typography, imagery, interaction, and visual rhythm.
7. Then implement.
Do not make isolated changes that create inconsistency elsewhere.
## VISUAL QUALITY
Prioritize:
- clear hierarchy
- sophisticated typography
- intentional whitespace
- strong composition
- restrained color
- consistent spacing
- subtle borders
- controlled contrast
- meaningful imagery
- purposeful interaction
- consistent visual language
Avoid generic AI/SaaS aesthetics:
- excessive gradients
- excessive glow
- glassmorphism everywhere
- random rounded cards
- giant floating blobs
- cyberpunk aesthetics
- meaningless particles
- excessive animations
- generic 3D illustrations
- stock-looking imagery
- arbitrary shadows
- decorative elements without purpose
## COMPONENT CONSISTENCY
If multiple components perform the same visual/function role:
- reuse the existing component
- extend the component
- or create a shared component
Do not create several slightly different implementations of the same UI pattern.
Before introducing a new value, check whether an existing design token already exists.
## UX STATES
Do not design only the happy path.
Consider:
- default
- hover
- focus
- active
- disabled
- loading
- empty
- error
- success
Interactive elements must communicate their state clearly.
## ACCESSIBILITY
Maintain:
- sufficient contrast
- visible focus states
- keyboard accessibility
- appropriate semantic HTML
- accessible labels
- usable touch targets
- reduced-motion support
Never sacrifice accessibility for aesthetics.
## RESPONSIVENESS
Never simply shrink desktop layouts.
Consider whether components should:
- stack
- reorder
- resize
- simplify
- change alignment
- change navigation behavior
- alter imagery
- change interaction patterns
for smaller screens.
## IMAGERY
Imagery is part of the brand system.
Do not treat images as interchangeable decorations.
Images should have:
- consistent art direction
- consistent lighting
- consistent color language
- intentional composition
- appropriate negative space
- strong cropping
- relationship to the surrounding UI
Avoid literal cybersecurity clichés unless strategically appropriate.
## VISUAL QA
For meaningful UI work:
1. Run the application.
2. Inspect the rendered page.
3. Capture/inspect the result at relevant viewport sizes.
4. Look for visual problems.
5. Fix them.
6. Re-inspect.
Never assume the UI is correct because the code compiles.
## DEFINITION OF DONE
A UI task is not complete merely because:
- the code compiles
- the component renders
- the requested element exists
It is complete when the rendered experience is visually coherent, responsive, accessible, and consistent with the site's design system (Maintenance Mode) or with the concept's own stated system (Exploration Mode).

## DE execution layer
Everything above this line is method and Tier 0 (accessibility, states, responsiveness, visual QA) and applies in every task mode. Root `.cursorrules` remains authoritative, read through `design/DESIGN-AUTHORITY.md`.
Tier 0 in every mode: canonical portal login `https://portal.digeratiexperts.com/portal/login` — never invent `//login`. Inspect rendered UI at 390 / 768 / 1440 at minimum. Never judge UI from source code alone. Functionality, routes, forms, SEO and integrations survive any restyle.
Maintenance Mode (default) — Tier 2 current system applies: preserve existing DE content, CTAs, nav, and stats unless DE explicitly approves removal; dark fields stay black/charcoal so `#D3126A` pops (`.cursor/rules/dark-field-accent-pop.mdc`), no purple-filled boxes; Visual System v2 layers on this foundation and HUD/evidence primitives are not invented ad hoc (`design/VISUAL_SYSTEM_V2.md`; ledger `docs/SITE-VISUAL-TASKS.md`).
Joe-decided (any mode: propose only, Joe ships): Blog/Journal amber; Store electric + the 14 category-pill hues — `.cursor/rules/blog-store-color-lock.mdc`.
Exploration Mode (Joe asks to redesign / rethink / explore / propose concepts): the Tier 2 items above are the benchmark, not the boundary; produce materially distinct concepts on an isolated branch, keep them out of production paths until Joe picks, and state what each concept keeps and changes.
